-**Time and Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-**Measured Performance:** Trimming the Fat [25] and ELMGS [18] remove 75% of Gaussians while maintaining high visual quality [S2].
-**Scaling:** Not given in the sources.
-**Strengths and Limitations:** It effectively removes Gaussians with no impact on training images [S1]. However, higher pruning thresholds can lead to the removal of fine details such as small leaves [S1].
-**When to use:** Use when Gaussians have zero sensitivity in color parameters, indicating no impact on training images [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Performance:** The sources note that a notable number of Gaussians (up to 15%) may have no impact on training images and can be safely eliminated [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Removes Gaussians that have no impact on training images, potentially reducing model size without significant loss in PSNR if thresholds are low [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Higher pruning thresholds can lead to the removal of fine details (e.g., small leaves) [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When aiming to reduce model size by removing structurally redundant Gaussians that do not contribute to the rendered output.
+*   **When not to use:** When preserving fine geometric details is critical, as aggressive pruning may remove them.
 
 ## Background
-Origin: Compressed 3D Gaussian Splatting for Accelerated Novel View Synthesis [S1].
-History: The method addresses the issue where Kerbl et al. [13] clamp negative direction-dependent colors, causing zero sensitivity for contributing SH coefficients [S1]. It was observed that up to 15% of Gaussians have no impact on training images [S1].
-Usage: Used in Compressed 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1].
+This method is described in "Compressed 3D Gaussian Splatting for Accelerated Novel View Synthesis" [S1]. It addresses the issue of redundant Gaussians in 3D Gaussian Splatting by identifying those with zero sensitivity to the total image energy.
 
